ntil you can actually make it  a reality for example a common why of  mine for language learning is to build  deep connections with native speakers in  their own language when i'm a beginner  cannot string two words together in a  language making such deep connection is  just impossible to bridge the gap  between a beginner me and the version of  myself who can actually achieve that  purpose i use the power of my own  imagination specifically i close my eyes  and visualize myself months or years in  the future after i've gotten past the  sit and study phase  and i'm comfortable enough to live my  life in the target language in my mind's  eye i watch myself as i make friends  using the language and smile laugh and  have fun alongside native speakers i may  even picture myself traveling within the  country or visiting famous landmarks  with new friends this is exactly what i  do with every new language i learn to  this day i can even remember the images  they conjured up years ago when learning  german before i had even ever set foot  in germany images that i thought of  every day before sitting down and you  know hitting the books the funny thing  is that now even after i've reached  fluency in german and spent lots of time  in germany i still find those images to  be immensely motivating here's how you  can use your why to build a powerful  visualization that can fuel your  motivation for ears first of all you  need to find your why think about the  kinds of things you want to do when you  are a skilled user of your target  language for me it's usually traveling  making friends but for you it may be  different find a quiet distraction free  place and sit down with a pen and paper  take a moment to close your eyes and try  to mentally picture yourself doing all  of those things you listed as part of  your why and the last step try to flash  out the mental picture as much as  possible and ask yourself questions like  who am i with  what am i doing  where am i doing this and what and once  your visualization is sufficiently vivid  write down a description of it in words  it can be as long as you'd like but mine  is generally around 200 350 words keep  your written visualization with you or  at least near your study place and  before you sit down to learn each day  read what you've written and try to  picture it again in your mind if you  complete the steps you'll have a  powerful source of motivation that will  pull you into your desired future you  may even find that one day your  visualization will come true as many of  mine have two focus on systems rather  than goals most beginner language  learners are consistently stuck asking  themselves one question over and over  and over again when am i going to  finally become fluent in my target  language i understand why this is  heck i used to be in exactly the same  way just like on any language journey or  any journey in general it's easy to find  yourself wondering are we there just yet  or  my decades of language learning however  i've learned that these kinds of  goal-based questions are not useful it's  impossible to say exactly when any  language learner will definitely reach  fluency so obsessing about it will not  do anyone any good instead of goal based  questions i found that it's much better  to ask  system-based questions while goal-based  questions have answers that are often  either difficult or impossible to know  system-based questions have useful  answers that you can actually come up  with on the spot for  immediate impact good system-based  questions for language learners include  for example questions such as when can i  next sit down to learn my target  language how long can i dedicate to my  language learning today how can i make  it easier to start my next learning  session what can i do the next time i  don't feel motivated to sit down and  learn all or most of these system-based  questions have answers that are  repeatable for example if you know how  long 