o need to skip  one day of learning you never skip two  days and risk breaking your learning  habit in many ways systems are more  powerful than goals because they keep  you focused on the ways in which you are  taking action towards your goal and make  the overall process of learning easier  smoother and more habitual three aim for  the goldilocks zone when you were a kid  did your parents ever tell you to finish  your vegetables whether we intend to or  not we often feel like we have to finish  the things we start simply because we've  been taught it's the right thing to do  we finish books we do not like we eat  meals we do not enjoy and we sit through  tv series that got stale halfway through  just so we can say we  finished what we started but while that  is often a useful rule in daily life  it's not always useful when learning i  can still remember when a couple of  years ago i was coaching a student who  was learning german like many of my  other students i had taught him how to  apply my bi-directional translation  method to the astronaut course books  which typically contain anywhere from 80  to 100 dialogues originally it seemed  that his heart was set on applying my  method to every lesson of the book from  start to finish  gradually though i noticed that his  passion for the asthma lessons began to  wane and one day during a lesson he had  with me he said um luca listen i i love  your method and i love austin mill but  i've had enough of these dialects and  what should i do and i think he expected  me to be upset a little bit or say  something along the lines of just just  stick with it you know just power  through and until the end and everything  will be fine but actually i did not  instead i i told him  he could draw a passimo right away and  move to another activity and another  resource and it did not have to finish  all the lessons of asamil so  why did i let him stop even though he  technically had a lot you know more  material that he could cover with asimo  because he no longer found it enjoyable  or challenging and enjoyment and and and  challenge should be the two things at  the heart of any learning activity you  do and any other learner should do at  any level to maintain motivation while  learning a language you should  constantly assess and reassess your  learning activities to ensure that you  find them fun rather than boring and  find them neither too easy  nor too difficult in other words you  want to make sure that every activity  that you do is in the language learning  equivalent of the goldilocks zone now if  you remember the tale of goldilocks and  the three bears you'll understand the  concept just as goldilocks did not like  her soup to be too cold nor too hot you  do not want your learning activities to  fall into the extremes of too easy too  hard or too boring though i'm not sure  there's anything wrong with a learning  activity that's too fun identifying  learning activities that fall within the  special zone also has a very powerful  impact on your motivation as well author  james clear noticed that when he wrote  if you find yourself feeling unmotivated  to work on a task it is often because it  has drifted into an area of boredom or  been shoved into an area of great  difficulty you need to find a way to  pull your tasks back to the border of  your abilities where you feel challenged  but capable so that's why i recommend  for you here to  maintain long-term motivation you need a  plan to consistently keep yourself in  the goldilocks zone and the best way to  do this in my opinion is to regularly  ask yourself if you find your learning  activities fun and challenging revise  your language learning plan every three  to six months establish meaningful  relationships this is something i teased  a bit when talking about my personal why  but it's powerful enough that i feel it  deserves its own point to stay motivated  over the long term seek out meaningful  relationships with native speakers  whether you are introverted or  extroverted i think you will agree that  human beings are social creatures we  derive much  if not all of our happiness and  well-being from engaging with people we  care about and who care about us as  beginner language learners it's easy to  feel like there's a wall between you  and any native speaker you might connect  with you either feel too  inexperienced or too uncomfortable to  engage with them and as a result of this  you retreat into things that feel more  comfortable but that are more difficult  to connect with at a human level at  least books videos apps and so on and  even if you're stuck in the sit and  study stage i recommend finding ways to  build personal connection into your  language learning life you can try  things for example such as visualizing  yourself connecting with native speakers  which i spoke about earlier or joining  online or in-person meetup groups about  your target language or culture you can  hire a tutor you can find a language  partner even as a beginner you can find  value and connection in all of these  things in my experience if you're lucky  enough to form a strong bond with a  native speaker that bond will act as a  powerful motivator that will spur you to  get better and better in the language  and on top of that you'll have a  wonderful source of feedback and  encouragement i've developed these  connections in nearly every single  language i've learned  through language learning i've met many  incredible people with whom i've shared  countless adventurous and life-changing  experiences and all of those things  together have created a wonderful source  of motivation that has kept me learning  for decades on end if you establish a  meaningful relationships with native  speakers and with people in general i'm  certain that you too can develop such an  endless source of motivation and there  you go you now have a powerful set of  four strategies you can put to use to  create and maintain motivation for ears  and ears to come this makes the end of  my three-part video series of how to  start learning any language first we  covered simple steps for getting started  then we covered the power of translation  and lastly we touched upon  motivation if you've watched all three  videos in the series so far thank you so  much and if not be sure to check in the  the description box for links  to the other two videos you will not  want to miss those ones and last but not  least if you've enjoyed what i have  shared in the series and would like to  dive deeper into my personal methods for  learning a language from scratch then i  invite you to check the description box  again for a link to my brand new online  course which will provide you with more  in-depth strategies for mastering any  language and as always thanks for  watching and happy language learning  [Music]  [Music]  you

